David Wade wrote:
> running the VMS Editor each character was echoed back to the user at
> Application not OS level. So each time a user typed a character their
> program needed  to be unswapped, and dispatched to, all so it could echo the
> character back to the user.


Not quite. The terminal driver has a lot of smarts built into it. While the
driver does do the echoing, it is possible to code a QIO to complete only
after X characters have been typed (for instance fill a line), or whenever an
escape sequence has been entered. 

Also, with shared images, one can save heaps of memory since the same piece of
code is used by all users as they type.

Here is a good example of "smart" coding. WPS-PLUS on a VT100 versus Data
General's WORD. (nothing like MS).

I was able to support 12 users on a microvax II on an RD54 drive. Not only
because of shared memory , but also because WPSPLUS used the terminal driver
in a smart way: let the user type and don't bother reformatting the document
until he moves down one line or press some control key, at which point, you
just reformat the line above to make sure it fits the margins etc.

But the DG version has more marketing humph in it: it reformatted the whole
document in real-time as you added text in a paragraph, reflowing everything.
End result: on the DG you needed the equivalenmt of a VAX9000 to support the
12 users that ran fine with WPSPLUS on a Microvax II.


