>
>It was.

It got beaten by the RISC machines, but they were not mainstream
until the late eighties. That the VAX managed to (almost) equal the
RISC machines in speed and keep the CISC instruction set was an 
impressive feat of engineering. 

Intel has also done a great deal of work with the '86 instruction
set, but it never was anything near the VAX in 'CISCyness', and Intel
has redefined performance a few times on the way.

Both of these CISC architectures has had staggering development costs
that would have been a lot smaller with a redefined instruction
architecture. But these acheivements are still impressive. 

>> > Its day was about 2-3 years before it was introduced, in that case. ;)
>>
>> A lot of those was used for number chrunching at universities.
>>
>
>At this it was great. Unfortunatley in many cases they were not used as
>number crunchers more as general purpose interactive machines. And this
>tended to not show them at their best performance wise. For example when
>running the VMS Editor each character was echoed back to the user at
>Application not OS level. So each time a user typed a character their
>program needed  to be unswapped, and dispatched to, all so it could echo the
>character back to the user. On a reasonable busy machibe this would result
>in the dreaded "Type Behind" so loved by users.
>
>> What were the alternatives ?
>>
>
>Small IBM machines such as the 4381 ?  PR1ME 9000 series?

This was the time perion of 'one hardware generation too early
for Peecees or RISC workstations'. I was one of those who went
with the Prime path; and the major reason for this was that Prime
was not DEC. 

As for the Prime 9000; I saw Prime go stale as a company somewhere
around the introduction of the 9955-II; where the trusty 9950 got another
mid-life extention kick. That was the clincher that the high end had
gone sour. 

The Prime series was just as CISCy oriented as the VAX; but there were
more of a tradition of supporting several instruction and memory
models.  The 9950 was the first ECL-imlementation of the standard
'50-series hardware, and it soon also had support for the somewhat
sleeker IX mode IA and MM. the 9950 was definately a VAX-killer.It was
also obvious that this ECL feat was a struggle for the rather small
organization of Prime.

Prime ran this series through a lot of generations, the 9950 rapidly
got a version 2 as the hardware fixes and microcode upgrades came in.
Then the 9955 and the 9955-II came, all with incremental fifths of 
extra performance. Then nothing. 

After a long while the 6xxx series materialized, but by then it was
far too late. I wonder why we never saw 2 and 4 processor versions 
of the 9955. Of the 5 major hardware generations Prime had this was
the only one where the top end was never filled in with a multiprocessor.

But the busses and the OS supported if quite nicely. 

Thus, Prime had a rather brief time of glory from around '82 to around
'86, where things went downhill. At least DEC lasted a lot longer.

-- mrr



