>>Funny you should mention this.  Back inthe days before the
>>demise of Prime or DEC I worked with both.  Prime almost
>>always beat the VAX on benchmarks (even when DEC fudged the
>>numbers)  but DEC still almost always won the procurements. 
>>Why??  Because DEC had much better marketing.
>>Too bad DEC didn't see what bad marketing did to Prime and learned
>>from their mistakes.

The demise of Prime was not just bad marketing. It was bad
management in a lot of areas. This was even clearly visible to
outsiders; especially in the takeover moves they started playing
with.

>Was it marketing or was it the support infrastructure that DEC
>had in place?  A lot of customers counted support higher than
>performance; they assumed they would get the performance when
>they submitted an SPR.

In Northwestern Europe; normally an outpost for computer service
performance, Prime equalled or surpassed DEC in service coverage and
responsiveness.Prime always had a clear focus on good hardware
engineering and support, and this showed. It was just that management
stinked, especially from '85 onwards.

>I never saw the "outside" of DEC; I was always under the impression
>that our marketing was a joke and just barely tolerated by the
>customers.  

In this time frame sales were large ($40k and up) and the sales were
pretty personal. The vendors were dependent on sales reps that pushed
the goods. This was a person to person thing. There was a reason these
reps made the kinds of money they did. The customers expected personal
sales, not mass marketing. If you want to sell you hire the right sales
folks, and let them loose. I never saw a lot of difference between DEC,
Prime, IBM, NCR; Sun, SGI, ND or others in this respect. 

Back then a service company with lots of software would bet its
existence on the computer company they used for their computing
platform. Last minute migrations and upgrades were normal when
computer vendors did not perform. Vendors tried their best to lock in
customers on peripherals, comms gear, memory etc.

We, the customers tried our best to obtain competitive hardware.

This was a game of trust and distrust. DEC and IBM had shown several
moves that led us to shun them like the plague; with hairraising
stories about total disregard for customers that had bet the shop on
their products.  The demise of the '10 was one of these stories. One
of many. ND and DG also made us very wary, as did the platform choices
from Wang.

With the smaller fry you had a lot more leverage as a medium to large
customer. Only, they learned this game as fast as anyone else.  And
they did so many exotic, irrational moves.  Sun and SGI also picked
this up. It was only with the internet age that this 'lock in the
customers growth' game went away.

-- mrr




