Brian Inglis <Brian.Inglis@SystematicSw.ab.ca> writes:

> VAX interrupt handling was also really "heavy" -- benchmarks with
> the same I/O device configurations showed that 11/780s could not
> handle as many interactive users or SCADA device interrupts as
> 11/70s and other I/O was no faster on the 11/780. This situation did
> not get better until terminal servers and LAT were available.

Not quite. All the vaxen except the 780 used direct vectors, and that
improved things a good bit. The biggest problems where the DWUBA and
the horrific overhead of the DZ-11 for terminals. All the disadvantges
of a 1 line per controller controller plus all the disadvantages of
multiple lines per controller :( A new version of the DH did not happen
till a lot later, in the DHV, then the DHU after VMS engineering had
beaten it up somewhat.

Also, MSCP controllers could run with very few interupts under load,
just as LAT overhead drops as load goes up. But CI780s where not cheap,
and UDA50s again invoked the curse of the DWs.

