In article <3E000CD6.F6EF630B@sasquatch.com>, pzachary <pzachary@sasquatch.com> writes:
>
>Well, the pdp-15 wasn't really a general purpose computer, more like a
>set of industrial process-control logic (a sort of roll-your-own) using
>rope memory and such, ISTR some of the larger and later ones
>incorporated a pdp-11/15 or somesuch as controllers.
>I guess you could have hooked a card reader to the controlling
>computer...
>
>Pavl_

This statement is totally wrong.

The PDP-15 was a follow-on to the PDP-9, which was a sort-of
follow-on to some earlier computers going back to the PDP-1.

It was absolutely positively a general purpose computer, and was
used in a great many areas: Telecommunications (as a switching
controller and as a message processor), Graphics (some of the
early CAD/CAM systems ran on it), scientific processing,
radio station automation (the CBC used it to control automated
station operation), database processing (hospitals ran MUMPS
on it), typesetting (many newspapers used it), and many other
applications.  And it did not use "rope" memory or logic.

It was similar to most of the other Digital products at the
time by being constructed of many "Flip-Chips" plugged into
a backplane: but the first PDP-11s, PDP-8s, and many
peripheral controllers were also built this way.

Perhaps this person was thinking of the hybrid computers: I believe
the PDP-14 was one such system.

