> Peter, I believe in you that, decserver 200&300s do this more than 1..
> But How can I find it..There are some paramerts on the DS200&300. For
> example Retransmit Count, but I dont know if it is the one, I am
> looking for..? Can you give me a resource for this ..?

I don't think this is configurable - it just happens. As I recall from
Decserver 100, and later Decserver 90, it requests at init, then after about
30 seconds, then 1 minute, 2 minutes, 4 minutes. With a Vax doing the MOP
load, by the time MOP was started, the request interval was 20 minures. It
loaded eventually.

As posted before, TCP/IP compatible servers also do bootp, so one thing you
could try is to run up a bootp server on a PC or something, and set it to
log ALL requests. You should see the Decserver requests, although they won't
be satisfied unless you have configures the server correctly.

> 
> 
> Hans, I think LANCP is being used with OPENVMS.. We have left the VAX
> machine for 1 years.. Instead of that, I have ALPHA Tru64Unix
> systems..I have found the following tools there..
> 
> ncl
> ccr
> mop_setup
> 
> 
> can I do something with those programs ..? I could not find TRIGGER
> command there..
> 

ncl is the Phase V (AKA Decnet/OSI etc) config program. Phase IV used NCP,
which had the trigger command. I don't know whether there is a Phase V
equivalent, but VMS retains part of NCP to do things like that.


mop-setup should be able to configure the mop server to be able to download
on request. It may be able to turn on messages from the mop server, which
would be useful.

It occurs to me, if your server isn't loading, whether the problem lies in
the network. These boot messages are broadcast, so routers will block them,
as will some bridges. (Bridge 90 can be set to block by protocol header,
tylically blocking cluster traffic.)

