> I thought Red Hat has SMP by default.

When installed.  The installer will see it, but the actual kernel does not
load-share.

> Both cpu are displayed on bootup by the bios?

BIOS is before that.  The computer should know it has two, otherwise it
probably doesn't.

> If you install a Windows 2000 does that recognise both cpu's?

Same deal, Windows 2000 will install using a single CPU first.  When it
detects you have an "ACPI Multiprocessor" PC and replaces the appropriate
parts of the HAL, it'll start using SMP (it usually does this on the last
leg of the install).

Windows 2000 on a P166 is pretty crappy.  Anything less than a PentiumII
or Celeron isn't worth using Windows 2000 for at all (stick with NT4).

