No kidding.  He's not really specifying whether he plans on doing a root
login with X (VERY bad idea considering what can be done in terms of X
windows attacks) or whether he's just mentioning that X has to run
partially as root and that he's using a normal user account (which is not
as bad, since the attacks, if any, would be limited largely to that user
account).

If the answer really is xhost, then the problem is *partly* solved.  If
you restrict it to a particular host like I suggested, then you wouldn't
have to worry about the above outlined issue.

